    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **no, you should not read anything significant into the fact that the panel was made up entirely of women.** \n\nHere is why, along with how to interpret the situation and what steps to take next:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why the Panel Composition Is Unlikely to Be Significant\n\n* **Both you and the successful candidate are women:** Because the position went to another woman, gender bias is very unlikely to be a factor in why she was selected over you.\n* **Civil service panel demographics:** In many public service frameworks, guidelines strictly discourage or prohibit all-male panels. Depending on senior leadership demographics and availability in that specific ministry or division, an all-female panel is often simply a natural outcome of scheduling rather than a deliberate or unusual statement.\n* **Standardized scoring:** Civil service recruitments are typically governed by rigid, merit-based competency frameworks. Panels must score candidates independently against predetermined criteria (e.g., behavioral questions, leadership competencies, and scenario tasks) rather than personal preference.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why the Other Candidate May Have Been Selected\n\nReaching the final shortlist of three confirms that your qualifications and experience met the standard for the role. At that stage, selection often comes down to:\n\n* **Specific strategic priorities:** A master’s in political science combined with \"experience leading international projects\" may have closely aligned with an immediate, specific priority of that division (e.g., cross-border negotiation, multilateral policy development, or stakeholder engagement).\n* **Competency scoring on the day:** Civil service interviews are heavily weighted on how well specific examples are framed using the required framework (e.g., STAR technique) during the interview itself, rather than raw years of experience on paper.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay for Feedback\n\nTwo weeks without written feedback is standard—and often even considered fast—for government HR processes. \n\nWritten feedback in the civil service typically requires:\n* Collating notes and scores from all four panel members.\n* HR or recruitment team review to ensure the notes comply with employment standards.\n* Formal sign-off before release.\n\nA delay of two weeks is almost certainly administrative inertia rather than an indication of a problem.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n* **Follow up politely:** Send a brief, professional follow-up email to HR or the panel coordinator reiterating your appreciation for the opportunity and asking if there is an update on the written competency feedback.\n* **Request the score sheet/matrix:** When you do receive feedback, ask for the specific scores against the competency framework so you can identify where the panel felt your interview responses were weaker.\n* **Keep the focus on competencies:** Avoid raising the panel’s gender composition. Bringing it up would likely be viewed as looking for grievances without basis, potentially harming your reputation within the ministry.\n",
